{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794471855, "title": "threejs/neon-implosion \u00b7 not-ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Create a vivid pointer interactive abstract neon art solar system particle implosion, a 10 second looping\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  animation in three.js, as a single web page.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[80.776, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/solar-implosion.html)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[85.964, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I wrote the page to `<workspace>/solar-implosion.html`. I haven't opened it in a browser, so none of it has\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  been run or checked visually. Open the file in a browser; it needs internet access because three.js loads\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  from a CDN.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The 10-second loop**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **0–1.5 s:** a neon solar system. A sun, 9 planets (three with rings), glowing orbit trails, an asteroid\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  belt and an outer dust halo, all made of about 80k particles.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **1.5–5 s:** everything spirals inward and collapses into a white-hot core. Bloom swells and the camera\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pushes in.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **5–9 s:** a shockwave blasts the particles outward, and they settle back into orbit.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Seamless loop:** every orbit and spin uses a whole number of turns per loop. The last frame matches the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  first.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Pointer**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Moving:** your pointer repels and swirls nearby particles, and a neon reticle follows it. The camera\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  also shifts slightly with the pointer.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Holding:** the force flips to pull particles toward the pointer, and the reticle turns magenta.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  A small progress bar in the corner shows where you are in the loop.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[89.976, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[118.211, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && python3 - <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[124.722, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I pushed the animation further in `<workspace>/solar-implosion.html`. I haven't opened it in a browser, so\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  none of it has been run or checked visually. A script applied the edits, and the only check I ran was a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `grep` confirming the new post-processing import is in the file.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What's new**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Density:** the sun and planets have more particles. A spiral galaxy of dust arms (40k particles) now\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  wraps the system. The total is roughly 190k particles.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Comets:** 14 comets follow tilted orbits. Each has a white-hot head and a long fading tail.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Colour cycling:** hues drift across the loop and shift with distance from the centre. The loop still\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  repeats exactly every 10 seconds.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Afterimage trails:** motion leaves glowing streaks behind it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Chromatic aberration and vignette:** the colour split intensifies at the implosion peak.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Shockwave rings:** two colour-shifting rings expand through the system after the burst.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Click shockwave:** pressing the pointer also sends a ripple through the particles from the cursor.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Holding still pulls them in as before.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  If it runs slowly, lowering the particle counts (the sun's 30000 and the galaxy's 40000) is the easiest fix.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'd also reduce the bloom strength if the centre blows out to white.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
